   "content": "<p>Socrates — Mahdi Ifadi</p><p>Socrates, who was among the famous and renowned sages of Greece, was born in Athens. This city was at one time the capital of Greece. It also had a university. Socrates’ father was a sculptor. In keeping with the ancestral profession, this unique man of his age too acquired practice in stone-carving, but at last he developed a passion for studying philosophy; since a talent of a high order was naturally present in his temperament, he took on the influence of philosophy very quickly.</p><p>In early life, as required by the law of the land, he had to join the army. In several battles he performed great deeds. He saved the lives of capable men like Xenophon and Alcibiades. For this reason both of them also loved him greatly. Xenophon, besides being a commander of the army, was also an author; his writings are of a special standard. Alcibiades was the son of a nobleman. Virtues of every kind were packed into him, and along with beauty of form, his excellent beauty of character was like borax on gold (an added grace).</p><p>After being freed from war, Socrates changed his former way of life. He observed simplicity in food and clothing. He published philosophical writings and urged his countrymen to observe their religion. Gradually a large group of sages began to benefit from his ideas. A series of teaching and learning began. In various gardens and on the bank of the river he used to explain the delicate problems of wisdom and philosophy to his pupils. He was very free in temperament and also an extremely good speaker. In the end his philosophical subtleties became a cause of envy and jealousy for his countrymen. A poet wrote a lampoon on him, the purport of which was that Socrates corrupts the morals of the youth of Athens and teaches boys to turn away from obeying their parents. On this very ground the court declared Socrates guilty. (continued on p. 12)</p>",
