   "content": "<p>The second day too passed in the same way, and we kept fast upon fast in starvation. Towards evening the chaudhri’s man brought milk and sweet rice and said: “Today we had a niyāz (votive offering); this is its food, and five rupees are of zakāt. Every year we give the zakāt of our goats, but this time we have given it in cash.”</p><p>This food and money seemed to me such a blessing as if I had been given a kingdom. Joyfully I told the whole story to Mother and kept giving thanks to God, but I did not know that the turn of misfortune had cast its shadow on the man’s mind, while womankind still stood just as before upon her ancient sense of honour.</p><p>So I saw Mother’s colour change. Despite the weakness of starvation she changed her look and said: “Shame on your honour! You have brought charity and zakāt and are pleased. Oh, it would have been better to die than this. Although we have been wiped out, our spirit has not died. To go out into the field and die, or to kill, and to take bread by the strength of the sword, is our work; living on alms is not our way.”</p><p>At these words of Mother I broke into a sweat and my hands and feet went cold with shame. I wanted to get up and return these things, but Mother stopped me and said: “If this is what God wills, what can we do? Everything must be borne.” Saying this, she kept the food, and after breaking the fast we all ate together. Five rupees’ worth of flour was bought, with which Ramadan passed safely and well.</p><p>After that we stayed six months more in the village, then came back to Delhi. On coming here Mother passed away, and I married off my sister. The English government has fixed a pension of five rupees a month for me too, on which my whole life now depends.</p><p>(Begumat ke Aansoo)</p>",
