   "content": "<p>Back towards Patriotism — Maulana Salahuddin Ahmad</p><p>Before the creation of Pakistan, the feeling with which the hearts of the Muslims of India were filled was the feeling of the unity of the millat (the Muslim community). And although this feeling completely erased patriotism (waṭaniyyat) from our thought and consciousness, it was this very feeling that ultimately became the cause of the creation of our national homeland. This is an astonishing riddle of history and a deep secret which cannot be solved with complete accuracy and certainty. In any case, as far as the present demands of our life are concerned, it is today not only fitting but necessary to decide what our national ideal is, what is the straight path to achieving it, and to what extent our patriotism and our millat can be helpful or opposed to it.</p><p>As I have just submitted, the rise of the feeling of millat among the Muslims of India, and then, as a result of this rise, the creation of a national homeland, apparently has the status of a historical riddle. About a quarter of a century before the creation of Pakistan, the thoughts of that great man who gave us the concept of Pakistan were continuously making the belittling of homeland and patriotism their pivot; but when the thinker of Islam, in the presence of his poetic inspirations, turned his attention for a short interval to the practical politics of the Muslims of India, he invited them to establish an Islamic homeland in north-western India. Here I do not wish to start the debate whether this invitation was for all the Muslims of India from Jamrud to Cape Comorin, or only for the inhabitants of those regions which were settled from the river Indus to the river Jamuna; and that dream which Tipu Sultan too had seen, and Abdul Hamid II too, and Jamaluddin Afghani too, and the thinker of Islam Allama Iqbal himself too — whose dream was going to be fulfilled? But one thing is quite clear and evident, and there is no room for any doubt in it, that the millat and the aims of the millat… (continued on p. 28)</p>",
