   "content": "<p>Ghulam Abbas</p><p>Born: 1909 · Died: 1982</p><p>Ghulam Abbas was born into a poor family of Amritsar. He received his early education at Dyal Singh High School, Lahore, and began his practical life in Lahore itself. He was the editor of various magazines. Then he joined the Department of Public Relations; some time later he joined the BBC and went to London. Ghulam Abbas was greatly influenced by Turkish literature. He was also interested in music; he was especially fond of playing the guitar. Besides this he was an excellent chess player. In recognition of his literary services he was awarded the Sitara-e-Imtiaz by the Government of Pakistan. Ghulam Abbas died in Karachi after a dignified, successful and creative life.</p><p>Ghulam Abbas was interested in Urdu literature from the very start, and in childhood he had read almost all the works of Ratan Nath Sarshar, Maulana Abdul Halim Sharar, Khwaja Hasan Nizami and Mirza Hadi Ruswa. He is counted among the modern short-story writers. He began writing stories when, because of the spread of English education, new values were growing in society and there was a state of conflict, as a result of which events were occurring that were unacceptable to a traditional society. Ghulam Abbas has depicted such a society very beautifully and in an unemotional manner. The subject of Ghulam Abbas’s stories is not new, but his manner of expression has given them novelty; his story “Anandi” can be cited as an example. In fact he makes the ordinary extraordinary. He is not subject to emotion. Whatever the subject of the story, his pen moves on with impartiality and reason.</p><p>Works: Anandi, Jaare ki Chandni, Kan Ras, Dhanak, Gondni Wala Takiya, etc.</p>",
