   "content": "<p>Groups of clerks pour out of the offices — typists, record-keepers, dispatchers, accountants, head clerks, superintendents — each type distinguishable by dress: cyclists in half-sleeved shirts, shorts and sola hats; pot-bellied babus under umbrellas with bundles of files under their arms (which they carry home every day hoping to solve problems in peace, but never even open); carefree young men with coat on shoulder and collar pinned; new recruits in cheap ill-fitting suits with several pens in their pockets.</p><p>Though most share one mother tongue, they speak a foreign language with affected accents, because they must use it all day with their officers. They are of every age: fresh lads three months out of school, and seasoned old hands with deep marks of spectacles on their noses who have watched the ups and downs of this road for twenty-five or thirty years, hunched at the neck, their cheeks rough from cheap razors. The walkers know to the step how far it is to their homes; everyone grumbles about irritable officers or incompetent subordinates.</p><p>Sharif Husain gets a seat in a tonga, gets down near the city gate and, instead of going home, walks to the Jama Masjid, around whose steps every evening sellers of second-hand and cheap goods set up a fair. He stops briefly by hakims, sadhus, amulet-sellers and street photographers and reaches the junk shops, full of once-fine objects now damaged — china, lamps, clocks, frames, gramophone parts, surgical instruments, a stuffed deer, half a statue of Buddha. In one shop he notices a piece of marble, apparently torn from some Mughal tomb or pavilion, about a foot and a quarter long and a foot wide… (continued on p. 70)</p>",
