   "content": "<p>The marble is so finely cut that he asks its price just out of curiosity. “Three rupees,” says the junk-dealer. Sharif Husain puts it down and walks on, but when called back, ashamed to admit he had only been curious, he offers one rupee — a price he hopes will be refused. The dealer agrees at once. Annoyed with himself for not saying twelve annas, he examines it again for a flaw that would let him back out, but it is flawless.</p><p>That night, lying alone on his rooftop, he thinks of a use for it: God’s ways are wonderful — perhaps his luck will turn, he will rise from second-grade clerk to superintendent, his pay going from forty to four hundred, or at least become head clerk; then he will leave the shared house for a small house of his own and fix this marble, engraved with his name, outside the door. The dream so fills his mind that the piece he had thought useless now seems something he had long been seeking.</p><p>When he first took the job he had burned with ambition, but after two years of fruitless effort his zeal had cooled. Now the marble stirs his hopes again. When the month ends and he is paid, the first thing he does is take it to a famous engraver, who cuts his name on it with elegant little vines in the corners. Seeing his name in such bold letters gives him a strange joy — perhaps the first time in his life. On the way home he longs to unwrap the newspaper and look again, but some shyness holds him back, as if passers-by might read his secret thoughts.</p><p>At the foot of his stairs he throws the newspaper away and climbs slowly, gazing at the lovely lettering. But at his upstairs door he realises for the first time in two years that there is no place outside it where any plate could be fixed — and in any case such plates are meant for big houses, by whose gates passers-by would see them… (continued on p. 72)</p>",
