   "content": "<p>He puts the nameplate in the doorless cupboard of one of his two small rooms. Each evening, coming home tired, he sees it first and its hopes ease his fatigue; when a colleague seeks his guidance his eyes shine with a sense of superiority; news of someone else’s promotion stirs longings; a kind glance from an officer intoxicates him for a week.</p><p>Until his wife and children return he lives in his daydreams, but once they are back, household cares close in and the bright pictures fade. The plate lies a year in the cupboard; he works very hard and tries his utmost to please his officers, but nothing changes. When his four-year-old son can reach the cupboard he moves it into his trunk under the clothes; when summer comes his wife clears the trunk for woollens and the plate goes into an old wooden box of broken frames, hairless brushes, useless soap dishes and broken toys.</p><p>He stops thinking much about his future, concluding that promotion comes by luck, not by hard work. Every second year his pay rises by three rupees, enough for the children’s schooling. After twelve years, when all ambition and even the memory of the plate have gone, his officers, in view of his honesty and long service, give him for three months the temporary post of a first-grade clerk on leave. Overjoyed, he walks home on foot to give his wife the news, buys a cheap writing table and a swivel chair at an auction, digs out the plate, washes it and props it on the table.</p><p>It is a hard time: to impress his officers he does twice the work of the clerk on leave, and even some of his subordinates’ work, poring over files till midnight — yet he is happy. Sometimes he wonders whether the clerk might extend his leave, fall ill, never return… But when three months pass, the clerk neither extends his leave nor falls ill, and Sharif Husain must return to his old post. (continued on p. 74)</p>",
