   "content": "<p>The following days are full of disappointment and gloom; after a glimpse of prosperity his state seems worse than before, and he grows listless and irritable — until his officers’ frowns soon bring him back into line. Years pass: his elder son is in class six, the younger in class four, the middle daughter learns the Qur’an, sewing and housework from her mother. The elder son takes over his father’s table and, since the plate takes up space and might fall, puts it back in the doorless cupboard.</p><p>Over the years the plate keeps changing places — cupboard, table, top of trunks, under the cot, in a sack, in the wooden box. Once it is put in the kitchen niche; smoke turns its white to yellow. Sharif Husain washes it and returns it to the cupboard, but soon it disappears again, replaced by big pots of red paper flowers given to his son by a friend, which make the whole room glow. After twenty years of service his hair is more than half white and his back bent; daydreams of prosperity now last only a moment before worries about his daughter’s marriage, his sons’ education and jobs drive them away.</p><p>At fifty-five he is pensioned. His sons now work — one in a railway goods shed, one as a typist — and with his pension the household lives comfortably on about a hundred and fifty rupees a month. Through thrift and his wife’s good management he marries off his elder son and his daughter with some pomp. He wishes to go on Hajj but cannot; for a while he frequents the mosques, then old age and illness keep him mostly in bed. Three years after retiring, rising from his warm quilt on a winter night, he is struck by the cold wind, catches pneumonia, and after four days in bed he dies.</p><p>After his death, while clearing the house, his elder son finds the plate in a sack. Seeing his father’s name, he weeps and gazes long at its calligraphy; then an idea makes his eyes shine. Next day he takes it to an engraver, has the wording slightly altered, and that same evening fixes it on his father’s grave.</p><p>(Anandi)</p>",
