   "content": "<p>Ahmad Nadeem Qasmi</p><p>Born: 1916 · Died: 2006</p><p>Ahmad Nadeem Qasmi’s real name was Ahmad Shah. He was born in the town of Angah in Tehsil Khushab. His father&#x27;s name was Pir Ghulam Nabi. He received his early education in his ancestral area and passed the B.A. examination from Sadiq Egerton College, Bahawalpur. After his marriage he began his career as a sub-inspector in the Excise Department.</p><p>Ahmad Nadeem Qasmi’s personality has many aspects: he was a poet of high rank, a distinctive short-story writer, an outstanding journalist and an excellent critic. His uncle Khan Bahadur Haider Shah had a deep influence on Nadeem’s personality; his uncle was a classmate of Allama Iqbal. In this way Nadeem developed an interest in poetry and literature. Nadeem also took an interest in journalism; he published several literary magazines, including Phool, Taleem-e-Niswan, Naqoosh, Sahar, Funoon and the daily Imroze. Indeed, Funoon became the hallmark of his personality. He also wrote humorous columns in newspapers under the name “Harf-o-Hikayat”.</p><p>Nadeem’s first story is “Badnaseeb But-Tarash”. Nadeem’s writing is simple and fluent. The subject of his stories is the problems and emotions of ordinary people living in villages. The emotion of love is a universal truth. Village people, who are hard-working and toiling and deprived of many of life’s amenities, are also moved by the emotion of love, and this very truth is Nadeem’s subject. His stories reflect real life.</p><p>Although Nadeem’s attention was mostly on story-writing and he wrote stories continuously, this does not diminish his poetic greatness. In his poetry there is classical elegance on one hand and a modern style and rhythm on the other; there is an awareness of contemporary consciousness. Some of his poems are very effective with regard to music. He wrote both nazm and ghazal and proved his ability in both, but his identity rests on the ghazal. His collection of verse “Dasht-e-Wafa” also received the Adamjee Award.</p><p>Collections (prose): Chaupal, Bagole, Tulu-o-Ghurub, Gardab, Aanchal, Sailab, Aable, Dar-o-Diwar, Sannata, Bazar-e-Hayat, Barg-e-Hina, Ghar se Ghar tak, Kapas ka Phool, Neela Pathar (short stories).</p><p>Collections (poetry): Dhadkanen, Rim Jhim, Shola-e-Gul, Dasht-e-Wafa, Muheet, Dawam, Lauh-e-Khak, Baseet, etc.</p>",
