   "content": "<p>Mothers — Ahmad Nadeem Qasmi</p><p>[Paragraph-wise summary — Ahmad Nadeem Qasmi (d. 2006) is in copyright; read the full story in the printed textbook, pp. 80–84.]</p><p>The neighbourhood could not remember a single day without a quarrel between the wives of Raja Sahib and Khwaja Sahib; if one was late, superstitious people looked anxiously at the sky. Their houses shared a wall, so a nail driven on one side cracked the plaster on the other. Once, after the usual midday quarrel, a slight earthquake struck in the evening; Begum Raja, thinking Begum Khwaja was dragging a bed next door, abused her through the window until their husbands told them to recite Ayat-ul-Kursi because of the earthquake.</p><p>Yet neither wife observed purdah from the other’s husband: when Raja Sahib ran out of razor blades, Begum Khwaja would hand him one; when Khwaja Sahib needed boot polish or a hot-water bottle, Begum Raja would pass it over. The wives fought so fiercely that it went as far as “May I see you widowed with these eyes”, yet a little later Raja Sahib would call through the window, “Khwaja Sahib, coming for a walk?”, and the two husbands would stroll off arm in arm, laughing.</p><p>The husbands treated the quarrels as routine and saw no point interfering. When an elder of the neighbourhood asked them to restrain their wives because the whole street was getting a bad name, Raja Sahib replied politely that it was a women’s matter — even two pots kept together clatter, and these were, mashallah, living women; Khwaja Sahib added, “living, waking and talking women”. In their fights there were few accusations and many curses, so the neighbours found little fun in them; the men lost interest altogether, and the women who rushed to their rooftops came away disappointed… (continued on p. 82)</p>",
