   "content": "<p>…because neither wife ever accused the other of any scandal or immoral taunt; the neighbourhood women had to listen to these quarrels the way a patient must eat food without salt or chilli. The quarrels began and ended for no reason — for instance, when Begum Raja’s son’s ball bounced through the window into Begum Khwaja’s bucket, Begum Raja shrieked that it had been soaked on purpose so the child would dirty his clothes and waste her soap and time, while Begum Khwaja insisted Begum Raja had aimed it at the bucket since the tap was off and the water-carrier charged two annas a skin.</p><p>Such quarrels rose to the height of “May Allah make your child die” — “My child is God’s trust; may your child die first, so I see you tear your witch’s hair” — threats to jump through the window and put embers on each other’s tongue or break each other’s legs; then both would glare, weep with anger, and go back to their housework. The quarrel usually began from Begum Raja’s side; Begum Khwaja’s only fault was that she seemed to be waiting for it and never once ignored her.</p><p>One day, strangely, Begum Khwaja came to the window with blood in her eyes and challenged Begum Raja herself: Begum Raja’s son had stuck a pencil into her darling’s thigh, the lead had gone in, and the child was crying himself sick — what if she plunged a knife into Begum Raja’s boy in return? Begum Raja answered she would chew her liver raw, and demanded to be shown even a scratch. Begum Khwaja set her child, eyes swollen from crying, in the window and bared his thigh. Begum Raja leaned close — and fell silent. She touched with a finger the spot where the sharp pencil had torn the skin; blood had seeped and dried, with a red ring around it… (continued on p. 84)</p>",
