   "content": "<p>Black-skinned — Shaukat Siddiqui</p><p>[Paragraph-wise summary — Shaukat Siddiqui (d. 2006) is in copyright; read the full story in the printed textbook, pp. 88–94.]</p><p>At night on a lonely road, stray dogs bark; a shadow flickers in a speeding car’s headlights, a painful human cry rises, and the car jolts and crashes into an electric pole. The driver, Durrani, sits stunned, then finds a big man lying face-down in a pool of blood. With no one in sight he drags the man to the roadside and tries to flee, but the car will not start. He thinks of running on foot, but the car itself would be full proof against him.</p><p>After about fifteen minutes a truck comes; Durrani stops it and asks to be taken to the police station. The truck crew see the black man in his blood and the smashed car. At the station Durrani takes the sub-inspector aside, explains, and has his younger brother bring the station wagon; they return with two constables. The man is still lying senseless but alive, though he has lost much blood; they take him to hospital.</p><p>Arriving home late at night, Durrani learns from the hospital that the wounds are not fatal, though one leg is shattered; untroubled by the accident, he lies awake scheming how to claim five thousand rupees from the insurance company for the car’s damage. Some newspapers report only that the man was Abdullah, a rickshaw driver walking home after returning the rickshaw to its owner, struck by a speeding car at the turn of Nicholson Road… (continued on p. 90)</p>",
