   "content": "<p>…the man looked at his face, choked with fright, and ran off making strange noises, dropping a bundle. Abdullah, startled too, picked it up: it was hot amritis (sweets). He hurried home and he and his wife ate them with relish. The next night, in drizzle and deeper cold, he waited long in the lane; at last a peanut-seller came, and Abdullah, instead of holding out his hand, said nasally, “Wait a moment, brother!” His frightful face, ghostly voice and the lonely night so terrified the man that he fell senseless; Abdullah calmly tied a seer and a quarter of peanuts in his shawl and went home.</p><p>These two incidents caused a sensation: people said the colonel’s ghost was now troubling passers-by, and fear gripped the neighbourhood. Abdullah exploited it, lurking in the dark lane and growling “Makhan tosh!” at whoever came; he now fully assumed the colonel’s ghost’s role. At first he contented himself with food, but when a victim fainted he would empty his pockets of cash. His boldness grew — he would grab people, frighten them with laughter, drag someone by the leg, block someone’s way.</p><p>In time passers-by stopped using the lane by the graveyard at night altogether, so Abdullah went out into other dark streets to collect his “tax”. By dusk the neighbourhood fell into dead silence; his face grew more frightful, his voice like a dying man’s. One night, after days without a “catch” — and his wife no longer going to Sakina Begum’s — they had starved for several meals. Patrol constables’ footsteps forced him to hide; at last, in desperation, he decided to try something new: he knocked softly at a house door… (continued on p. 94)</p>",
