   "content": "<p>Ashfaq Ahmad</p><p>Born: 1925 · Died: 2004</p><p>Ashfaq Ahmad was born in Uttar Pradesh (India). He received his early education in his ancestral area. Some time before the creation of Pakistan he migrated to Lahore and settled there permanently. Here he resumed his education and did an M.A. in Urdu literature from Government College Lahore. His wife Bano Qudsia, a famous Urdu novelist, was his classmate at Government College Lahore. After completing his education he joined Radio Azad Kashmir. Some time later he left this job, came to Lahore and began teaching Urdu at Dyal Singh College, Lahore. About two years later he went to Rome, where he was appointed Urdu newscaster at the radio and at the same time taught Urdu at a university in Rome. During his stay in various countries he obtained diplomas in Italian and French, and a diploma in radio broadcasting from New York.</p><p>On returning home he started his own magazine named “Dastan-go”. At the same time he also wrote features for the radio. During this period his famous radio programme “Talqeen Shah” began to be broadcast. This programme, pointing out social shortcomings and contradictions in a light manner, was extremely popular among listeners and was broadcast for about thirty years. He was also Director of the Central Urdu Board (this institution is now called the Urdu Science Board). For some time Ashfaq Ahmad also served in the Federal Ministry of Education.</p><p>Ashfaq Ahmad had begun writing and compiling in his student days. His stories were published in the monthly “Phool”. He wrote hundreds of dramas for Radio Pakistan and Pakistan Television. His important subjects were literature, philosophy, psychology and Sufism. Because of his expression of human emotions and feelings and his promotion of love for humanity, he was extremely popular among listeners and readers.</p><p>Works: Zavia, Aik Muhabbat Sau Afsane, Gadariya, Man Chale ka Sauda, Hairat Kada, Safar dar Safar, Tota Kahani, Aik Muhabbat Sau Drame, etc.</p>",
