   "content": "<p>…while the others, as he put it, were “being ruined”. Coming out of the maze of “A is equal to X”, they knew nothing of why Lord Wavell suddenly left or why Mountbatten, the nineteenth viceroy, had come. Every night, dawn call to prayer and temple bell only reminded them of a small exam; but every heartbeat of Lalu’s told him of a great test, and his face shone with a contentment that would put the dawn to shame.</p><p>He often skipped school; his brother whispered: “Don’t tell Mother — he goes to the Muslim League office now; she’ll beat him.” Gradually the brother too joined the “conspiracy”. One day the two left in the morning and did not return till the next morning; their mother wept all night that her sons were gone and only their corpses would come. Next day the master heard the news and went straight back; at four o’clock he appeared holding each boy by the neck and handed them to their mother: “Here — your sons were playing leader and making Pakistan.”</p><p>The mother turned on the master for filling their heads with this notion; flustered, he said he had never told them to join processions right before exams — but he had found Liaquat Ali Khan’s procession under way and these “Mughal princes” riding elephants, garlanded, raising slogan after slogan. She begged him to beat them till all their leadership left through their noses. Lalu cast a rebellious look: “Let leadership go, let life go — Pakistan [he said ‘Paqistan’] will be made!” — a major change: he now said Pakistan with a qāf. The master beat both of them soundly, then taught the rest while twisting Lalu’s ears: “Will Pakistan be made for ignoramuses like you? Are you fit for Pakistan?” When he left, Lalu began to chant in his stammer that exams did not matter: “We’ll die, but we’ll make Pakistan.”</p>",
