     "text": "The narrator’s family came to Pakistan in 1947; there was no news of Lalu, though she thought of him every 14th of August — “Le ke rahenge Paqistan, ban ke rahega Paqistan.” Then, at a wedding in Karachi, a child said some people outside were asking for her. Under the canopy sat a row of young men — old classmates and playmates — all standing up saying “Recognise us?” Lalu was among them, reminding her of Liaquat Ali Khan’s procession and the master’s beating. Recovering from her surprise she asked where he was and what he did. “I am a bus conductor.” “You’re joking.” “Not at all. What harm is there? This Pakistan of ours is just a big bus…” (continued on p. 116)",
