   "content": "<p>Now he came out openly: he went to the Muslim League office, pasted posters on the city walls and sang, in his stammer, “Such is our leader, Muhammad Ali Jinnah.” When his mother asked his father to warn him, the father laughed: whom could they stop now? This was the voice of the times, the voice of the people. Lalu still stammered, but “Pakistan” and “Quaid-e-Azam” he took great pains to pronounce correctly — that was his tribute of devotion.</p><p>When the exams were over the children again felt the changing seasons; a strange atmosphere grew as if everything, like Lalu, were waiting. Then that night passed, the cock crowed at dawn, and the familiar call rang from the minarets in a new tone: “Allahu Akbar!” That was the morning they heard the new news. Everyone congratulated Lalu, who stood not with head raised like a conqueror but with head bowed; all were amazed, all excited — except Lalu, who had known all along that this day would come. On the morning of 14 August 1947 the narrator felt not awe of new history but as if a singing waterfall were coming down from on high and suddenly the ocean had turned and rushed forward, roaring, beneath the waterfall.</p><p>The narrator’s family came to Pakistan in 1947; there was no news of Lalu, though she thought of him every 14th of August — “Le ke rahenge Paqistan, ban ke rahega Paqistan.” Then, at a wedding in Karachi, a child said some people outside were asking for her. Under the canopy sat a row of young men — old classmates and playmates — all standing up saying “Recognise us?” Lalu was among them, reminding her of Liaquat Ali Khan’s procession and the master’s beating. Recovering from her surprise she asked where he was and what he did. “I am a bus conductor.” “You’re joking.” “Not at all. What harm is there? This Pakistan of ours is just a big bus…” (continued on p. 116)</p>",
