   "content": "<p>Fulfilling a Will — Farhatullah Baig</p><p>God forgive him, Maulvi Waheeduddin Saleem too was a strange thing. Think of him as a gem that lay uncut for years; when it was cut, its lustre grew and it was valued by the discerning — at that very moment it snapped and broke.</p><p>Fame too, like Ghalib&#x27;s ode, suits no one these days. Here a name grows, there one dies. One steps out of the ranks and becomes the target of the arrow of fate. The fashion is for leaving: today this one went, tomorrow that one. Maulvi Nazir Ahmad went, Shibli went, Hali went, Waheeduddin went. These days even dying has become a strange dying. Formerly life was likened to a lamp: the wick burned, the oil was spent, and when the oil ran out the lamp flickered and trembled, the flame began to sink, and at last it slowly went out. Now, in place of the lamp, life has become an electric lamp: here you press the button, there it is pitch dark. Azmatullah Khan died in this way, Maulvi Waheeduddin departed in the same way. Now let us see whose turn it is. Two or four lamps are burning in the assembly of Urdu; they too will go out with a click at any moment, and after that, there is only Allah.</p><p>I have been in Hyderabad for a long time. Maulvi Waheeduddin too had been here for years, but we never met. He had no leisure to meet; I had no leisure to meet. At last when we did meet, the Maulvi Sahib was sitting ready to die. Last year Maulvi Abdul Haq Sahib drew me to Aurangabad for the college function. When I reached the Hyderabad station to set off, what do I see but that the station is packed with people going to Aurangabad — students and masters too; some going out of need, some going without need; some real guests, some uninvited guests. In short, half the train was filled by the passengers for Aurangabad… (continued on p. 122)</p>",
