     "text": "…on it a dirty white sheet. Two or four mouse-nibbled pillows and a rotten quilt lie there. On the walls hang one or two pictures from cigarette advertisements and three or four old calendars. In the cupboard in the wall opposite are five or six teacups without handles or spouts, chipped saucers and one or two tea tins. In the room opposite, on pegs, hang two or three sherwanis and two or three caps; below lie two or three pairs of old, creaking shoes. There you have the summary of the Maulvi Sahib’s household. The Maulvi Sahib sits; before him are two braziers — water on one, milk boiling on the other. Tea is being made; he is drinking it himself and giving it to others. A lump of salt lies beside him; he makes tea, drops the lump of salt in, gives it one or two stirs and takes it out. That was his only occupation all day. There were no cooking pots in the house — how would food be cooked and who would cook it? Who knows where he went to eat?",
