   "content": "<p>Exercise</p><p>1. Write answers to the following questions.</p><p>(a) When and how did the Maulvi Sahib and Farhatullah Baig meet?</p><p>(b) Describe the Maulvi Sahib&#x27;s appearance in your own words.</p><p>(c) What views did Farhat express about the Maulvi Sahib&#x27;s temperament?</p><p>(d) What misunderstanding arose at the annual function of the Hyderabad college?</p><p>(e) What excuse did the Maulvi Sahib give for his thrift?</p><p>(f) Explain this sentence: “and the Maulvi Sahib would fall out with someone or other.”</p><p>(g) What principles did the Maulvi Sahib rely on to coin technical terms?</p><p>2. Explain the following sentences.</p><p>(a) Before the oil runs out the lamp flickers and trembles, the flame begins to sink and at last slowly goes cold. Now, in place of the lamp, life has become an electric lamp: here you press the button, there it is pitch dark.</p><p>(b) But when does my tongue stop! I said: “Maulvi Sahib! Why are you worried? In the name of God, go ahead and die; I will write it in an essay.”</p><p>(c) The tongue was his own; he did not know how to defer to anyone; he would cool his heart by giving people a piece of his mind.</p><p>(d) Fearing drought, he kept filling his coffers in times of plenty.</p><p>(e) The wider his vision grew, the narrower his heart became.</p><p>3. Fill in the blanks with suitable words:</p><p>(a) He stepped out of the ranks and became the victim of the arrow of ________ .</p><p>(b) An old man is coming towards me taking long ________ .</p><p>(c) Everyone praised his quick ________ .</p><p>(d) Men of merit have always remained in ________ circumstances.</p><p>(e) I drew a picture of the Delhi of that ________ .</p><p>(f) God gives the sugar-eater ________ .</p>",
