   "theory": "Idiom (muhāwara) and everyday usage (rozmarra)\n\nWhen two or more words are used in a figurative rather than literal sense, it is called an idiom (muhāwara) — for example, “āṅkheṅ dikhānā” (to glare at), “āṅkhoṅ se girānā” (to lower in esteem), “nau do gyārah honā” (to vanish, take to one’s heels), “tāre ginnā” (to pass a sleepless night), etc. Rozmarra is the name for the everyday speech of native speakers. In rozmarra words are used in their literal meanings. An idiom comes within the bounds of grammar whereas rozmarra is above grammar. Likewise an idiom does not change, whereas rozmarra keeps changing according to native speakers.",
