   "content": "<p>…everyone respected Qamar and cheerfully bore his bitter words. It is an accepted fact that Zia’s literary fame owed much to Qamar, whose sincere efforts raised him to the seat of spiritual guidance.</p><p>Zia was president of the Daira-e-Adabia and Qamar its secretary-general and the man in charge. Qamar’s awe kept the author from ever speaking freely to him; he respected the author as a Syed almost to embarrassment, yet scolded him sharply for the slightest error. Though not formally his teacher, Qamar truly taught the author to hold a pen in prose: he had him write reports of mushairas, corrected them and pointed out errors of language, idiom and usage; he also strictly taught manners of sitting, speaking and reading in company, and would mock the young men for ill-matched clothes — “What will people say, that poets and writers don’t know how to dress?” In every way he spared no effort in their training, so that the author can proudly say (with Ghalib’s half-line): “Whatever we became, we became by staying there.”</p><p>Such was the encouragement that the author, not even a beginner in that literary world, was soon made librarian by Qamar — an honour that thrilled him, so he devoted himself eagerly to arranging the small library. The next year he was given the duty of deputy secretary, and he had to work very hard to prove himself worthy of it.</p><p>Zia’s personality embodied the aesthetic. A godly man and disciple of the seat (gaddī) of Batala Sharif, after performing Hajj he became sajjāda-nishīn; his disciples multiplied from Peshawar to Karachi and he was cut off from the literary world, only occasionally composing a naʿt or manqabat. Whenever they met he would say, “Recite your ghazals,” and listen with delight, swaying and praising.</p><p>After a heart attack he was unwell for many days. The day before leaving for a mushaira in Karachi, the author went with Khatir to see him. He looked very cheerful, with the same glow and playfulness; once in form he chattered as if he had never been ill, recalling past events one by one with peals of laughter — mushaira jokes, his own follies, friends’ rivalries, quarrels and debates — as though the book of the past had opened. His talk was worth seeing; afternoon turned to evening, and when they rose to leave he bade farewell reluctantly.</p><p>When the author returned from Karachi four or five days later, the children gave him the heart-rending news of Zia Sahib’s death the moment he entered the house; he could not believe that the radiant, fragrant Zia had left them forever.</p><p>Zia was a colourful chapter of the literature of the Frontier, a school of thought and a movement; his literary and scholarly services are unforgettable. He lit the lamps of learning and literature here, and their light and the fragrance of that evergreen flower will always brighten and perfume hearts.</p><p>(Album)</p>",
