   "theory": "The Pir of Muridpur — Patras Bokhari\n\nMost people are surprised that I never mention my home town. Some are also amazed that I never go to my home town any more. Whenever people ask me the reason, I always evade the question. This gives people all sorts of suspicions. Someone says: there was a case against him there; that’s why he is in hiding. Someone says: he was employed somewhere there; he was charged with embezzlement, and had to flee. Someone says: his father does not let him into the house because of his misdeeds. In short, as many mouths, so many tales. Today I am going to remove all these misunderstandings. May God grant you readers the grace to be fair.\n\nThe story begins with my nephew. In appearance my nephew is no different from ordinary nephews. All my virtues are present in him, and besides, being of the new generation, he shows some surplus qualities; but he has one attribute that has never appeared in our family with such intensity to this day. It is that he respects his elders, and in his view I am nothing less than a god of learning and art. Why this mania has entered his head? The only reason I can give is that even in the very highest families this is sometimes seen. I have sometimes seen the sons of cultured families respect their elders so much that one begins to suspect them of low birth.",
