   "content": "<p>…“One doesn’t need to be an M.R.C.P. and F.R.C.S. and tap here and there with a tube to give it. I looked from Landi Kotal to Landhi and saw nobody more loving than you, more content himself and gladdening others; so I took a ticket and came to you — the rest was an excuse to scrape the rust off my spirit. The days I spent with you have added that much to my life. May God keep you ever cheerful and kind to me. Asking forgiveness for the trouble I caused must be one of those Lucknow formalities beyond an ignorant man like me.</p><p>“May this illness be ruined — the cup of life is spilling before it is full. Even while dictating this letter I run short of breath; for fear I cannot even cough properly. Your sister-in-law (bhābī) starts crying; hidden from me she rains tears every now and then with thunder and lightning. I keep telling her, my fortunate one, until I am quite unconscious I am not a man to admit defeat to illness. Bisharat Bhai! For such a man there is a very bad word in Pashto. Last week I began building a new house on University Road; in its hall there will be room for fifty Peshawari or a hundred Karachi poets to sit cross-legged.</p><p>“All else is well. To everyone, according to rank, salaams, prayers, love and scolding.”</p><p>Bisharat left for Peshawar by the first train.</p><p>(Aab-e-Gum)</p>",
