     "text": "Iqbal is a figure of whom not only Asia but the whole world is proud. He began writing poetry in his school days, but it was after his tour of Europe that his themes were truly settled. He had studied world history, so he was aware of the causes of the Muslims' decline. Through his poetry he gave the message of movement and action. His philosophy of selfhood (khudī) and his concept of the true believer (mard-e momin) became a guiding light for Muslims. Unlike the traditional Urdu poets, Iqbal introduced Urdu poetry to new subjects, ideas, style and philosophical thought. His observation was extremely powerful, and he had mastery of scene-painting. In short, Iqbal's poetry is the best blend of tradition and innovation. Because of his immense poetic qualities he is called the Poet of the East (Shāʿir-e Mashriq), the Sage of the Ummah (Ḥakīm-ul-Ummat) and the Pulse-reader of Nature (Nabbāẓ-e Fiṭrat). Iqbal also had mastery of Arabic and Persian; he has used Qur'anic verses very beautifully in his poetry. Besides this, a large part of his poetry is in Persian; Iqbal's universal ideas are found mostly in his Persian verse.",
