   "content": "<p>Scenes of Dawn — Josh Malihabadi</p><p>[Stanza-wise summary — Josh Malihabadi (d. 1982) is in copyright; read the full poem (seven six-line stanzas) in the printed textbook, pp. 200–201.]</p><p>Stanza 1: The face of dawn is a soul-refreshing sight — a heart-pleasing Kashmir, a paradise to the eye. Every flower’s face is moist with the sweat of beauty; everything carries a meaning and an effect. On every side blazes the flame of the sun’s face; in every humble speck is the flame of Sinai.</p><p>Stanza 2: The quivering of the stars, the smiling of the specks, the flowing of the springs whose melody enchants; in the sky the clash of white and black, the storm of radiance and the surge of songs; the flying tresses of the dawn breeze scattered on the branches — or are they a fairy’s wings?</p><p>Stanza 3: The spreading of fragrance, the blossoming of buds, the dim moonlight, the shimmering of the sea; the fragrance of fresh roses under the starlight, the swaying of greenery and the waving of fields; branches meet branches, and the dawn breeze says, “It is the festival of dawn.”</p><p>Stanza 4: The coolness of the wilderness, the colourfulness of the desert, the green valley and the clear pond; on the forehead of the sky the smiling star, the forest paths and the flowing river; heaps of flowers on every side of the garden, and the faces of flowers washed with dew.</p><p>Stanza 5: The lights of God in the soul, that true dawn, a beauty that makes every eye a lover; a simplicity in keeping with human nature, the golden horizon, the East brimming with light; the song of David in the birds’ voices and the effect of Joseph’s shirt in the breeze.</p><p>Stanza 6: The fresh rose-petal and the delicacy of dew, a laughter that is the stuff of reality; the radiance of idols, the adornment of the temple, the ascetic’s sight and the Brahmin’s grace; cries rising from the breast of the conch-shell, and the voice of the azan immersed in praise of God.</p><p>Stanza 7: This is the hour when the Master draws near to His servants; hearts become intoxicated and sorrows pass away; mercy spreads and rewards rain down — at such a time rest is in no way fitting. Whatever delight there is in weeping, the joy is in sighs: O soul, give up self, for “God” is near.</p><p>(Ref.: Diwan-e-Josh, first edition, compiled by Amar, Urdu Academy, Qaisar Bagh)</p>",
