   "content": "<p>Majeed Amjad</p><p>Born: 1914 · Died: 1974</p><p>Abdul Majeed Amjad was born in Jhang, Punjab province. At first he received education in Persian and Arabic from his maternal grandfather, Noor Muhammad. He studied up to the B.A., then joined the Rationing Department and worked in that department all his life. Besides this he was also editor of a semi-official magazine, “Urooj”. Majeed Amjad&#x27;s life passed in extreme distress and hardship; all his life he was beset by various problems, which gave his nature a certain steadiness and gentleness. Majeed Amjad&#x27;s name was taken with great respect in literary circles, but because he stayed mostly in Sahiwal for his job and because of his natural dervish-like disposition, he could not gain much popularity at the public level. Spending most of his life under the open sky, far from the bustle of the city, gave his poetry an earthly foundation; the background of his poetry is the land and the love of the land. He gained his real fame after his death.</p><p>Majeed Amjad began writing poetry at an early age, but the collection of his poetry was published quite late. He was basically a poet of the naẓm. Since his whole life passed in sorrow, despair and distress, his poetry too has an atmosphere of heaviness of heart. He made not only personal anguish the subject of his poetry, but also kept seeing life in a wide perspective; so when he gives contemporary issues the form of poetry, they take on a collective colour as well as being individual. Besides this he also made experiments in form and introduced the Urdu naẓm to a new manner and rhythm.</p><p>Collections of poetry: Shab-e-Rafta, Taq-e-Abad, Ungint Suraj.</p>",
