   "content": "<p>Ghazal — Allama Iqbal</p><p>Those whom I used to seek in the heavens and on the earths</p><p>turned out to be the dwellers of my heart’s dark chamber.</p><p>O Majnun, have you ever looked at yourself too?</p><p>For like Laila you too are among those seated in the litter.</p><p>The months of union fly past like hours,</p><p>but the hours of separation pass like months.</p><p>O boatman, will you stop me from drowning?</p><p>For those who are to drown, drown even in ships.</p><p>The wave of their breath can relight an extinguished candle —</p><p>O God, what lies hidden in the breasts of the men of heart!</p><p>Do not ask about these wearers of the patched cloak; if you have devotion, look at them:</p><p>they sit holding the White Hand (of Moses) in their sleeves.</p><p>For love, seek a heart that can break:</p><p>this is the wine that is kept in delicate crystal flasks.</p><p>Become manifest and show them your beauty some day;</p><p>for a long time you have been talked of among the subtle-sighted.</p><p>Be silent, O heart; it is not good to cry out in a crowded gathering;</p><p>courtesy is the first of the proprieties of love.</p><p>That I should think ill of them — that cannot be from me,</p><p>for I myself, Iqbal, am among my own fault-finders.</p><p>(Bang-e-Dara)</p>",
