   "content": "<p>Faiz Ahmad Faiz</p><p>Born: 1910 · Died: 1984</p><p>Faiz Ahmad Faiz was born in Sialkot and received his early education there. After his B.A. he did B.A. Honours in Arabic and then an M.A. in English and Arabic. Faiz&#x27;s practical life was very varied and eventful; for some time he also served in the army. He was arrested in the Rawalpindi Conspiracy Case and everyone was sure he would be sentenced to hanging, but he was given two and a half years&#x27; imprisonment. Faiz&#x27;s first collection, “Naqsh-e-Faryadi”, was published in 1941. Because of his poetry and humanitarian ideas he also received the Lenin Peace Award.</p><p>Among the poets connected with the Progressive Movement, Faiz Ahmad Faiz holds a very high and unique place. Faiz had a special gift for image-making; he was skilled in verbal portraiture. He is wholly a poet of aesthetics; he used artistic devices such as melody, imagery, metaphor and simile with great skill. Faiz was well acquainted with the traditions of Urdu poetry and was also familiar with Persian and English poetry. His poetry begins with romance and intuition, but the hard and bitter realities of life soon fix its permanent path. Besides personal pain he feels within himself the grief and suffering of the whole of humanity, and so his poetry becomes a beautiful blend of love and reality.</p><p>Collections of poetry: Naqsh-e-Faryadi, Dast-e-Saba, Zindan Nama, Dast-e-Tah-e-Sang, Sar-e-Wadi-e-Sina, Sham-e-Shehr-e-Yaran, Mere Dil Mere Musafir, Ghubar-e-Ayyam.</p>",
