   "content": "<p>Ahmad Faraz</p><p>Born: 1933 (as printed) · Died: 2008</p><p>Syed Ahmad Shah Ahmad Faraz was born in Kohat. His father&#x27;s name was Syed Muhammad Shah Barq, who was himself a very good poet; in this sense Faraz opened his eyes in a literary environment. Ahmad Faraz passed his B.A. from Edwardes College Peshawar and then did his M.A. in Urdu and Persian from Peshawar University, and for some time was associated with teaching at that university. Besides this he was Director of the Academy of Letters and head of the National Book Foundation. Ahmad Faraz believed in freedom of expression, and for this reason he had to face severe hardships under military governments, but he never compromised on his principles — so much so that he returned the “Hilal-e-Imtiaz” award received in 2004 on the ground that the government was openly violating human rights. Likewise he was so grieved at the conversion of the public library in Kohat into a plaza that he willed that after his death he should not be buried in Kohat.</p><p>Perhaps no one else&#x27;s poetry has gained as much popular acceptance as Ahmad Faraz&#x27;s. Writing verse in “sahl-e mumtaniʿ” (deceptively simple style) and then imprinting it on the minds of common and elite alike was an extremely easy task for Ahmad Faraz. His verse has a degree of reflection along with freshness. Protest against social injustices has been the theme of almost every poet, but in Ahmad Faraz this colour is distinct from all. Thirteen collections of his poetry have been published.</p><p>Collections of poetry: Tanha Tanha, Dard-e-Ashob, Nayaft, Shab-e-Khoon, Mere Khwab Reza Reza, Janan Janan, Be-Awaz Gali Koochon Mein, Nabina Shehr Mein Aaina, Sab Awazen Meri Hain, Pas-e-Andaz-e-Mausam, Khwab-e-Gul Pareshan Hai, Ghazal Bahana Karoon, Ae Ishq-e-Junoon Pesha.</p>",
